@startuml Topic8_MultiAgent_Cost_and_Fit
title Topic 8 - Multi-Agent Cost & Fit\n(Default is ONE agent; multi-agent must EARN its cost)

skinparam defaultFontSize 13
skinparam roundcorner 12

start

:Considering multiple agents for a task;

#LightCyan:Recall the COST MULTIPLIERS;
note right
  Baseline = plain chat turn = **1x** tokens
  Single agent in a loop      = **~4x**
  Multi-agent system          = **~15x**
  Costs don't overlap - they STACK.
end note

partition "WHERE THE 15x COMES FROM (3 stages)" {
  :1. Orchestrator overhead\n(planning + delegating, before real work);
  :2. Parallel fan-out\n(each subagent repeats setup + reasons alone);
  :3. Result stitching\n(orchestrator merges outputs = another model pass);
}

#Yellow:Apply the 3-QUESTION FIT TEST;
if (Q1: Split into PARALLEL directions\nthat barely touch each other?) then (NO)
  #Pink:Use a SINGLE agent;
  stop
else (YES)
  if (Q2: Does each piece fit its OWN context\nwithout shared state passed back & forth?) then (NO)
    #Pink:Use a SINGLE agent;
    stop
  else (YES)
    if (Q3: Valuable enough to justify a bill\nseveral times higher?) then (NO)
      #Pink:Use a SINGLE agent;
      stop
    else (YES to ALL THREE)
      #LightGreen:Multi-agent has a real shot -\nuse it, and match models to roles;
      note right
        Soften cost by MATCHING MODELS TO ROLES:
        * Opus 4.8 -> orchestrator (planning/merging)
        * Sonnet 4.6 / Haiku 4.5 -> scoped workers
      end note
      stop
    endif
  endif
endif

:== GOOD FIT vs POOR FIT ==\n* GOOD: breadth-first work - many INDEPENDENT lines\n  of inquiry (e.g. research many sources at once)\n* POOR: tightly-coupled work - each step depends on\n  the last, all need the SAME shared context;

:== THE CODING TRAP (exam favorite) ==\nA codebase LOOKS big, so people assume multi-agent.\nBut files depend on each other; changes must stay\nconsistent. That COUPLING means a single agent often\nfits coding better. SIZE != PARALLELISM.\n\nFraming: default is ONE agent. Multi-agent is the thing\nyou JUSTIFY, never the thing you assume.;

@enduml